WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.142
For this guided practice, the goal is department-level performance.

00:00:04.142 --> 00:00:11.057
The requirement says every department must appear, even departments with no admissions or no staff.

00:00:11.057 --> 00:00:15.652
That tells us the departments table should be the preserved population.

00:00:15.652 --> 00:00:19.542
We LEFT JOIN admissions and medical staff to departments.

00:00:19.542 --> 00:00:26.157
Because a department can have several admissions and several staff members, the detail rows can multiply.

00:00:26.157 --> 00:00:31.122
That is why the counts use DISTINCT event identifiers.

00:00:31.122 --> 00:00:35.367
Run the query and look for the department with zero activity.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:40.557
It should still appear. That is an important validation of the LEFT JOIN.

00:00:40.557 --> 00:00:44.992
Then inspect a busy department and compare the counts with the underlying records.

00:00:44.992 --> 00:00:49.787
This style of query is common in management dashboards.

00:00:49.787 --> 00:00:54.812
A company may want every region listed, even if a region had zero sales.

00:00:54.812 --> 00:00:59.012
A university may want every academic program listed, even if no students enrolled this term.

00:00:59.012 --> 00:01:04.962
The dashboard should not make zero-activity groups disappear.

00:01:04.962 --> 00:01:10.417
Read the query in sections: preserved table, first relationship, second relationship, grouping level,

00:01:10.417 --> 00:01:17.062
and measures. Breaking a long query into those logical pieces makes it much easier to understand.
